# Gun Fight sound generator -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# Colin Douglas Howell's netlist of Gun Fight's sound generator, every part labelled as on
# the schematic. The four effect switches are the real transistor switches (FAST_SWITCHES
# off), driven from the CPU's sound latch, whose bits arrive here as ports. MAME models
# the zener D304's noise as a source in series with it; here D304's anode and Q302's base
# are one node, as on the board.
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/midw8080/nl_gunfight.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(gunfight). Licence CC0-1.0 by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders Colin Douglas Howell (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/midw8080/nl_gunfight.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 7: #define USE_FRONTIERS 1 -> 0 (solver partitioning only)
# line 8: #define FAST_SWITCHES 1 -> 0 (a speed option)
# line 439: SYS_NOISE_MT_N(NOISE) is MAME's noise source in series with the zener it models the noise of; its two ends are one node here
# D304 written 1N5239, where MAME gives its model (D(BV=9.1 IBV=0.020 NBV=1)) -- MAME's file: 'a 9.1-volt zener diode (D304, a 1N5239)'
# Stand-ins that are not parts (the nets they drive are ports): CLOCK(NCLK), LOGIC_INPUT(I_LEFT_SHOT), LOGIC_INPUT(I_RIGHT_SHOT), LOGIC_INPUT(I_LEFT_HIT), LOGIC_INPUT(I_RIGHT_HIT)
#
# Not compared with the sheet. MAME's sheet, 'Gun Fight Sound Generator Detail P.C.
# 597-907E', is in none of the Gun Fight documents the site holds (read 2026-10-20): the
# 'Gunfight Schematics' PDF (doc d66a80e04858) has the mother board, the game logic board
# -- on which the sound generator is a dashed block -- and the cabinet wiring; the manual
# and parts catalog list no board parts. The catalogue's 'Game Logic and Sound (597-907E)'
# PDF (doc 8eaf458a433e) was never fetched, and an archive.org copy exists. What the site's
# sheets do show agrees where they reach: the four triggers enter the sound block from the
# 7404 at H6 (L.SHOT on pin 2, R.SHOT on 12), as MAME's comment says. The supply is printed
# '+16V' on the wiring diagram and '+15V' on the mother board schematic for the same pin;
# MAME uses 16V. The manual names six pots (A-B hit, C-D shot, E-F master, left and right),
# which by this circuit are R110, R210, R123, R223, R103 and R203 -- an inference, not a
# reading.
#
# Not compared with the sheet. MAME's designators, values and pin numbers are taken as
# MAME has them; the checker shows the netlist is internally consistent (every pin exists,
# no two outputs fight, nothing floats), which is not the same as matching the board.


value R104 30K
value R105 30K
value R106 1K
